8. REQUEST FOR QUOTATION (RFQ)

Customers should be able to request a quotation instead of directly purchasing stone.

An RFQ may contain:
- Customer
- Project
- Requested material
- Finish
- Thickness
- Quantity
- Dimensions
- Required service
- Delivery location
- Required date
- Customer notes
- Attachments

RFQ statuses:
- DRAFT
- SUBMITTED
- UNDER_REVIEW
- QUOTED
- CANCELLED

Important rule:
An RFQ does not reserve inventory.


9. QUOTATION MANAGEMENT

A Sales Agent reviews the RFQ and prepares a commercial quotation.

Quotation information:
- Quotation number
- Customer
- Project
- Sales Agent
- Creation date
- Expiration date
- Items
- Unit prices
- Quantities
- Discounts
- Taxes
- Transport
- Processing services
- Payment conditions
- Commercial notes
- Total amount

Quotation statuses:
- DRAFT
- SENT
- ACCEPTED
- REJECTED
- EXPIRED
- CANCELLED

A sent quotation should not be silently overwritten.

If pricing or quantities change after sending, the system can create a quotation revision.


10. QUOTATION REVISION

Example:

Q-2026-0042
Revision 1
Revision 2
Revision 3

Each revision preserves:
- Previous prices
- Previous quantities
- Previous terms
- Date
- Person who changed it

This provides a complete commercial history.


11. CUSTOMER QUOTATION ACCEPTANCE

The customer can:
- View quotation
- Download quotation
- Accept quotation
- Reject quotation
- Send a comment or negotiation request

When a quotation is accepted, the system must:

1. Verify quotation status.
2. Verify that it has not expired.
3. Check stock availability.
4. Reserve required stock.
5. Create an order.
6. Link the order to the quotation.
7. Mark the quotation as accepted.
8. Record the operation in the audit history.

One quotation should create only one order.


12. ORDER MANAGEMENT

An accepted quotation becomes a sales order.

Order information:
- Order number
- Customer
- Project
- Source quotation
- Order date
- Items
- Prices
- Quantities
- Reserved inventory
- Delivery address
- Payment status
- Order status
- Notes

Recommended order statuses:
- PENDING
- CONFIRMED
- PREPARING
- READY
- PARTIALLY_DELIVERED
- DELIVERED
- CANCELLED


13. ORDER SNAPSHOT

The order must preserve the commercial information that existed when it was created.

For every order item, store the commercial snapshot:
- Material name
- Finish
- Thickness
- Quantity
- Unit price
- Discount
- Tax
- Total

If catalog prices change later, old orders must remain unchanged.


14. STOCK RESERVATION

When an order is confirmed, inventory is reserved.

Example:

Travertine Beige

On Hand: 240 m²
Reserved: 80 m²
Available: 160 m²

New order requires:
85 m²

After reservation:

On Hand: 240 m²
Reserved: 165 m²
Available: 75 m²

The stone is physically still in the warehouse, therefore On Hand does not decrease until dispatch or delivery.


15. INVENTORY RESERVATION RECORD

Each reservation should be traceable.

Reservation information:
- Order
- Inventory item
- Quantity
- Reservation date
- Status

Reservation statuses:
- ACTIVE
- RELEASED
- CONSUMED

This makes it possible to know exactly which order is consuming available stock.


16. STOCK MOVEMENTS

All physical inventory changes must be recorded.

Movement types:
- INITIAL_STOCK
- PURCHASE_RECEIPT
- STOCK_IN
- STOCK_OUT
- TRANSFER
- ADJUSTMENT_IN
- ADJUSTMENT_OUT
- DAMAGE
- RETURN
- ORDER_DELIVERY

Every movement contains:
- Material
- Quantity
- Source
- Destination
- Reference
- Reason
- User
- Date

Stock should never be changed without traceability.


17. ORDER CANCELLATION

If an order is cancelled before the material leaves the warehouse:

- Active reservations are released.
- Reserved quantity decreases.
- Available quantity increases.
- The order is marked as cancelled.
- The cancellation reason is recorded.

The physical On Hand quantity remains unchanged.


18. DELIVERY MANAGEMENT

UNIVMAR should manage customer deliveries.

Delivery information:
- Order
- Customer
- Delivery address
- Planned date
- Actual date
- Vehicle
- Driver
- Items
